tonight we take a closer look at the  security clearance situation involving  the president's son-in-law and senior  advisor correspond to Jillian Turner  reports on what happens now after the  access downgrade for Jared Kushner  nothing that has taken place will affect  the valuable work that Jared is doing  senior officials across the  administration claim Jared Kushner's  foreign policy portfolio isn't in  jeopardy now that he's lost his  top-secret security clearance they also  insist that his efforts to broker a  Mideast peace deal won't be impacted I  can't see any change on the importance  of that or his work on that issue but  former senior national security  officials in the Bush and Obama  administrations tell Fox News Kushner's  downgrade from top secret to just secret  will impact his ability to do his job  they say he'll no longer have access to  the nation's most sensitive information  in addition to being barred from  president Trump's daily intelligence  briefing which often includes key  information for MIDI sources Kirchner  may also be excluded from national  security council meetings in which he's  supposed to play a key role they are  usually conducted at the top-secret  level so they will have to figure out  some kind of workaround to accommodate  mr. Kushner or exclude him from some  portions of some of the meetings Kushner  also won't be able to see top secret  intelligence generated by the CIA and  the NSA information like the real names  locations and methods of American spies  in foreign countries information that  intelligence community insiders refer to  as the crown jewels because of their  extraordinarily high value one senior  national security adviser to President  Bush says the primary job of a foreign  policy adviser is to take a vast  sometimes overwhelming amount of  information and distill it for the  president but now with his access to  information severely limited it's  unclear whether mr. Kushner will still  be able to do this Brett  Jillian thank you
 

